<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Os Lusíadas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1572</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as The Lusiads</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Os Lusíadas (The Lusiads) is the national epic of Portugal, written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-de-camoes">Luís de Camões</a> and published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> in 1572. In ten cantos of ottava rima it tells of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a>'s voyage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1497–1499 and, through it, the history of the Portuguese people, mixing classical gods with recent events. It is the most celebrated work of Portuguese literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/os-lusiadas.jpg" alt="Os Lusíadas" width="320" height="483" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Os_Lus%C3%ADadas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
